%N A397038 Sum of the quotients floor(k/n) over the distinct entries k of the n X n multiplication table.
%C A397038 This is the distinct-product counterpart of the quotient distribution A393470, which counts ordered factor pairs rather than distinct product values.
%H A397038 Frédéric D. W. Heidenthal-König, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18960358">Euclidean Decomposition of the Multiplication Table</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A397038 Frédéric D. W. Heidenthal-König, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19102497">The Support of the Euclidean Decomposition of the Multiplication Table</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%F A397038 a(n) = Sum_{k in {i*j: 1 <= i,j <= n}} floor(k/n).
%F A397038 Equivalently, a(n) = Sum_{k=1..n^2} floor(k/n) * A394016(n,k), where A394016(n,k) is the support indicator.
%e A397038 The distinct entries of the 3 X 3 multiplication table are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9. Hence a(3) = floor(1/3) + floor(2/3) + floor(3/3) + floor(4/3) + floor(6/3) + floor(9/3) = 7.
%o A397038 (Python)
%o A397038 def a(n):
%o A397038     products = set()
%o A397038     for i in range(1, n + 1):
%o A397038         for j in range(1, n + 1):
%o A397038             products.add(i * j)
%o A397038     return sum(k // n for k in products)
%o A397038 n = 50
%o A397038 print([a(k) for k in range(1, n + 1)])
%o A397038 (PARI) a(n)=my(u=[1..n]); vecsum(Set(concat(vector(n,i,i*u)))\n) \\ _Andrew Howroyd_, Jul 20 2026
